<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/8th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Roncevaux Pass</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">778</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Roncevaux</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Roncevaux Pass was fought in 778 in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>'s rearguard was ambushed and destroyed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basques">Basques</a> during the withdrawal from a failed Spanish campaign. The event demonstrated the geographic limits of the Carolingian military machine. It was later celebrated in legend, notably in the medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> of Roland, and is part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire</a>'s military history.</p></div>
